WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.300
Picture a bus driver

2
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.740
in Lantern Bay,

3
00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:03.420
walking home

4
00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:04.100
past a brand

5
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:05.430
new apartment building.

6
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.600
It's called Harbor Walk.

7
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:08.700
Building homes like these

8
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.640
costs a lot.

9
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.680
Land, materials, workers,

10
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:13.700
loans, permits,

11
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:15.570
it all adds up.

12
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.620
to cover those costs,

13
00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:18.600
new homes often

14
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:19.870
need higher rents.

15
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:21.120
But many

16
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:21.820
of the people who

17
00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:22.980
keep a city running,

18
00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.140
like teachers, nurses,

19
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:26.140
bus drivers,

20
00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:26.940
earn less than

21
00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.240
those rents need.

22
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.780
That space between what

23
00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.240
a home costs what

24
00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:32.060
people can pay

25
00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:33.420
has a name,

26
00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.900
the affordability gap. So,

27
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:36.520
how do you close

28
00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:37.780
a gap like that?

29
00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.160
Usually, with help.

30
00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:39.860
Next, we'll see

31
00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:40.990
how cities help.

